---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const title = "Founder OS — Changelog";
const description =
  "Every change to Founder OS since day one. A living log of how a plain-text operating system for running several businesses got built, one small change at a time.";

// The four-part series, for linking entries to where they are written about.
const PART1 = { label: "My New Operating System for Work", href: "/blog/my-new-operating-system-for-work" };
const PART2 = { label: "My Operating System, One Month Later", href: "/blog/my-operating-system-one-month-later" };
const PART3 = { label: "The Real Test of a System", href: "/blog/the-real-test-of-a-system-is-handing-it-to-someone-else" };
const PART4 = { label: "The System Is Not Its Tools", href: "/blog/the-system-is-not-its-tools" };

// Newest first. A living log; dates are when the change went in.
const changelog = [
  {
    date: "2026-07-02",
    phase: "Design",
    title: "Named the layers, not the tools",
    items: [
      "Reframed the whole system from three named tools into four swappable layers: memory (the shared read-write surface, split into long-term context and working RAM), the model, the interface, and dictation. The tool in each slot is just the current pick; the architecture is indifferent to which.",
      "Named dictation as its own layer at last. Voice was called out everywhere as 'by voice' but the thing that makes it true, the dictation tool that turns speech into text the model acts on, was never named. It is now.",
      "Relabelled the two memory tiers on the overview diagram (long-term memory and working memory) so the shape matches the language.",
    ],
    readMore: [PART4],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-06-24",
    phase: "Discipline",
    title: "Done means verified, not pushed",
    items: [
      "Tightened the definition of done: shipping something is not finished until the result is checked live, not just sent. Added after a silent build failure let a 'shipped to production' claim sit false for a day.",
      "Built this public overview and changelog of the system itself, with two diagrams of how it works.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-06-23",
    phase: "Milestone",
    title: "Handed the system to someone else",
    items: [
      "The first install for someone who is not me, which is the real test of whether it is a method or just my personal habit.",
      "Did it as an interview, not a copy: learned how they actually work, then shaped the same skeleton to fit, a different messy inbox, a different set of projects, a nudge default tuned to what they drop.",
      "Gap-analysed what their first version was missing against mine (the accountability layer, automatic backup, the Monday refill) and deliberately held those for a second pass.",
      "Wrote the whole thing up as a three-part series: the build, a month in, and the handoff.",
    ],
    readMore: [PART3],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-06-17",
    phase: "Routines",
    title: "Monthly reconciliation routine",
    items: [
      "A written routine that reads each card statement, splits every line business versus personal, reconciles against known expenses, and runs four passes: recurring subscriptions, category trends, unknown charges, and big-ticket items.",
      "Wired into the email triage so a statement landing in the inbox is picked up and handed off on its own.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-06-15",
    phase: "Routines",
    title: "Email triage routine",
    items: [
      "At each session start it sweeps the inbox, pulls out the actions, updates the right contact and conversation records, drafts the replies I owe into the working doc, and labels each message handled so nothing is processed twice.",
      "One hard guardrail, written into the rulebook: it drafts, it never sends.",
    ],
    readMore: [PART2],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-06-11",
    phase: "Infrastructure",
    title: "Cheaper sessions",
    items: [
      "Wired a context-engineering tool into the agent through a hook, cutting the token cost of routine operations sharply so longer working sessions stay cheap.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-06-05",
    phase: "Discipline",
    title: "A writing guide and a live dashboard",
    items: [
      "Added a writing guide: a long list of the tells that make text read like a machine wrote it. Everything I publish is now checked against it, automatically and on demand.",
      "Built a local dashboard that reads the to-dos and conversations and renders the day's agenda in the browser, refreshing on its own and starting itself on login, for a glance without opening a session.",
    ],
    readMore: [PART2],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-06-03",
    phase: "Discipline",
    title: "The triage pass",
    items: [
      "Added a triage pass to both the session-start and session-end checklists: the agent reads the notes I scribble into files between sessions (delegated, done, parked) and files each one where it belongs before it builds the agenda.",
      "It immediately started earning its keep, catching tasks marked obsolete, moving stalled items to the backlog with a trigger for when to revisit, and reconciling annotations across the to-dos and conversations.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-06-01",
    phase: "Design",
    title: "A self-review and a logging rule",
    items: [
      "Made the done log immediate: a finished item is written the moment it is done, as the outcome rather than the file change, because context can compact mid-session and erase the morning's work.",
      "Wrote a full assessment of the system into its design doc: the six things genuinely working (the two-layer split, the four-state people file, nudge dates, typed relationships, the session model, structured contact files) set against six structural problems still worth solving.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-05-31",
    phase: "Contacts",
    title: "Contacts became a queryable network",
    items: [
      "Added a typed relationships block to every contact file, 40-plus at the time: who introduced whom, who works with whom, who can introduce me to whom, who shares an org or a community. The network is now answerable by query instead of by memory.",
      "Borrowed from Garry Tan's GBrain: prose descriptions of who knows whom are invisible to a query, typed edges are not.",
      "Documented the schema in the rulebook so every new contact file carries the block automatically.",
      "Added the 'to reply' state to the people file, finishing the four-state shape: reaching out now, owe a reply, waiting on them, not yet contacted.",
      "Stood up a standalone design doc for the system, and parked GBrain's graph traversal as a thing to revisit once the contact files cross a few hundred.",
    ],
    readMore: [PART2],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-05-30",
    phase: "People",
    title: "The people layer and its cadence",
    items: [
      "Built the delegation file: a checklist of what is handed to whom and by when, no narrative.",
      "Restructured the daily flow, pulling the last people-items out of the to-do list (the old waiting and follow-up sections were deleted) and putting a nudge date on every one of the fifteen items I was waiting on.",
      "Added a weekly pull to the queue and a Monday refill prompt, so the list of people to contact does not rot into a graveyard.",
      "Fixed the session-start agenda to read the real day and time, which had been getting the day of the week wrong.",
      "Wrote the daily-files rules into the rulebook so each file's one job is explicit.",
    ],
    readMore: [PART2],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-05-29",
    phase: "Sharing",
    title: "First attempt to document it for others",
    items: [
      "Wrote a full setup guide for the system and shared it, the first sign it might transfer to someone else.",
      "Carved out a dedicated improvements backlog for OS design work, kept separate from real tasks, and captured the delegation file as the next thing to build.",
      "Applied the same pattern sideways: stood up a small sibling ops repo for a podcast, where pushing a transcript triggers automatic content generation through CI.",
    ],
    readMore: [PART3],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-05-28",
    phase: "Accountability",
    title: "Numbers, not feelings",
    items: [
      "Documented a hard cashflow baseline against the goal in the review doc, so the fortnightly review runs on real figures rather than a sense of how the week went.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-05-27",
    phase: "Cadence",
    title: "The daily summary and the CRM",
    items: [
      "Created the daily summary as a new file: one narrative paragraph per project, and made the session open by writing yesterday's summary first, so I start already oriented.",
      "Declared the contact system the CRM: individual files, the index, and the communities map together do the job, no separate tool.",
      "Added calendar guardrails to the rulebook, and started sequencing dependent to-dos explicitly so a blocked item shows what it is waiting on.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-05-26",
    phase: "Contacts",
    title: "A pipeline for the contact backlog",
    items: [
      "Loaded the full phone-contact export into a working sheet with structured columns and a freeform comment field, then set up a voice-driven workflow: scroll, dictate row numbers and notes in batches, and the agent turns the output into proper contact files.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-05-23",
    phase: "Foundations",
    title: "People split from tasks",
    items: [
      "Renamed the catch-all file to a dedicated conversations file and restructured the to-dos, separating people-facing actions from my own work for the first time.",
      "Added a rule to verify any name against the full contact export before creating a new contact file, locked into both the rulebook and memory.",
      "Created focused rulebooks for each of the six areas, all on absolute paths, so a working session can start from anywhere.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-05-21",
    phase: "Guardrails",
    title: "Hard rules and the first index",
    items: [
      "Email allowlist: the agent can only send to approved addresses, and anything else needs explicit approval.",
      "Calendar rule: every event the agent creates leaves attendees able to edit it.",
      "Built the top-level navigation index for the whole system, and a context file for each of seven properties so that side of life reads the same way as the projects.",
    ],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-05-20",
    phase: "Foundations",
    title: "The session loop, backup, and profile",
    items: [
      "Worked out how context and to-dos should be captured: a context file per project, a separate operational folder for the day, a session-end hook that updates every touched file, and a backup of the whole thing to GitHub.",
      "Added a README explaining the structure and the session flow, and a personal profile file holding the durable facts (background, which email to send from in which context, handles, location).",
      "Wrote the deployment and git practices into the orientation doc: pull first, the deploy workflow, the repo map, the naming convention for pushes.",
    ],
    readMore: [PART1],
  },
  {
    date: "2026-05-19",
    phase: "Foundations",
    title: "Day one",
    items: [
      "Settled on three plain tools and never left them: an Obsidian vault of markdown files, Claude Code as the agent that reads and writes it, and the terminal it all runs in. No custom app, nothing to lock into.",
      "Restructured everything into folders mapped to six life goals, with each project nested under the goal it serves and code repos separated from plain content.",
      "Renamed the Obsidian vault and set up the accounting folder, the first pieces of the reference layer.",
      "Ran a catch-up review covering the previous five months and added a metrics layer to the review template, so future reviews would have numbers to stand on.",
      "Wrote about it publicly for the first time, which is what eventually turned a private habit into something worth handing over.",
    ],
    readMore: [PART1],
  },
  {
    date: "before",
    phase: "Origins",
    title: "The practice that came first",
    items: [
      "Before any of the folders existed, there was a fortnightly review: what moved, what did not, and an honest assessment of why. The whole system was eventually built to feed that one habit, and to make it impossible to lie to.",
    ],
    readMore: [PART1],
  },
];
---

<div data-theme="cogniswitch" class="min-h-screen bg-base-100">
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} sideBarActiveItemID="founder-os">

  <div class="py-6 md:py-16 max-w-2xl mx-auto px-1 md:px-0">

    <header class="mb-14 space-y-3">
      <p class="font-sans text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-teal-600">Founder OS</p>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl md:text-4xl font-normal leading-tight tracking-tight text-base-content">Changelog</h1>
      <p class="font-serif text-lg leading-relaxed text-base-content/60">
        Every change since day one, most recent first. The whole thing is plain markdown in an
        Obsidian vault, run by Claude Code from the terminal, built in small steps. This is the
        record of them. New entries land as it evolves. For the story behind it, read the
        <a href="/blog/my-new-operating-system-for-work" class="text-teal-600 hover:underline">series</a>,
        or start at the <a href="/founder-os" class="text-teal-600 hover:underline">overview</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="space-y-12">
      {changelog.map((entry) => (
        <div class="border-l-2 border-base-content/10 pl-6">
          <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3 mb-1 flex-wrap">
            <span class="font-sans text-sm text-base-content/40">{entry.date === "before" ? "earlier" : entry.date}</span>
            <span class="font-sans text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-teal-50 text-teal-700 border border-teal-200">{entry.phase}</span>
          </div>
          <h2 class="font-serif text-xl font-normal text-base-content mb-4">{entry.title}</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-2.5">
            {entry.items.map((item) => (
              <li class="font-serif text-base leading-relaxed text-base-content/75 relative pl-5">
                <span class="absolute left-0 top-1.5 w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-teal-500"></span>{item}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          {entry.readMore && (
            <p class="font-sans text-sm text-base-content/45 mt-3 pl-5">
              In the writing: {entry.readMore.map((post, i) => (
                <Fragment>{i > 0 ? ", " : ""}<a href={post.href} class="text-teal-600 hover:underline">{post.label}</a></Fragment>
              ))}
            </p>
          )}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

  </div>

</BaseLayout>
</div>
